## Step 2: Identify the given projection matrix
The given projection matrix is $\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} \frac{13}{14} & -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{3}{14} \\ -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{5}{7} & \frac{3}{7} \\ \frac{3}{14} & \frac{3}{7} & \frac{5}{14} \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1}$.

## Step 3: Use the formula to find the normal vector
We know that $\mathbf{P} = \mathbf{I} - \frac{\mathbf{n}\mathbf{n}^T}{\mathbf{n}^T\mathbf{n}}$. By rearranging, $\frac{\mathbf{n}\mathbf{n}^T}{\mathbf{n}^T\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}$. Thus, we can find $\mathbf{n}$ by calculating $\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}$ and then finding the vector that, when multiplied by its transpose and divided by the dot product of itself, gives this result.

## Step 4: Calculate $\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}$
$\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P} = \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} \frac{13}{14} & -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{3}{14} \\ -\frac{1}{7} & \frac{5}{7} & \frac{3}{7} \\ \frac{3}{14} & \frac{3}{7} & \frac{5}{14} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{14} & \frac{1}{7} & -\frac{3}{14} \\ \frac{1}{7} & \frac{2}{7} & -\frac{3}{7} \\ -\frac{3}{14} & -\frac{3}{7} & \frac{9}{14} \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1}$.

