The matrix $\mathbf{I} - \mathbf{P}$ is a scalar multiple of $\mathbf{n}\mathbf{n}^T$. We notice that the entries in each row are proportional to the entries in the other rows, indicating that each row is a scalar multiple of the others. Specifically, the first row $\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{14} & \frac{1}{7} & -\frac{3}{14} \end{pmatrix}$ can be used to find the normal vector $\mathbf{n}$.

## Step 6: Find the simplest form of the normal vector
To simplify, multiply the vector by the least common multiple of the denominators to get integer components. The least common multiple of 14 and 7 is 14. Thus, multiplying the first row by 14 gives $\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & -3 \end{pmatrix}$.

